A Modular Framework for Simulating Attacks and Defenses in Federated Learning
Project Overview & Story
This project is a sophisticated and highly modular simulation framework for exploring the security and privacy landscape of Federated Learning (FL). It is built using Python, PyTorch, and the Flower FL framework.

The project began as a simple FL simulation for image classification. Its capabilities were progressively expanded to serve as a robust research tool. The development story followed a logical path of introducing threats and then building countermeasures:

Privacy Attacks: We first introduced Gradient Inversion (GI) attacks, where a curious server could reconstruct a client's private training data from its shared updates. This included simple attacks like DLG and advanced, state-of-the-art methods like GGL and GradInversion+.

Integrity Attacks: We then introduced integrity attacks, where malicious clients attempt to corrupt the global model. This includes untargeted Model Poisoning (e.g., scaling attacks) and targeted Data Poisoning (backdoor attacks).

Standard Defenses: To counter these threats, we implemented a suite of well-known defenses, including client-side perturbations (Clipping, Differential Privacy) and server-side robust aggregation (Median). We also implemented transport-layer security simulations (AES encryption, chaotic obfuscation).

Adaptive Defense: The core of the project became the MyDefense agent, a custom, adaptive defense mechanism on the server. Unlike standard defenses, this agent analyzes each client update in real-time based on two key criteria: Contribution Utility (is the client helpful?) and Data Leakage (is the client leaking too much information?). Based on this analysis, it can reject harmful updates or trigger countermeasures like chaotic obfuscation for specific clients in subsequent rounds.

Generalization: To test the robustness and flexibility of the framework, it was generalized to support multiple data types:

Binary Images (Casting Defect Dataset)

Multi-class Images (CIFAR-10)

Numerical/Time-Series Data (Sensor Dataset)

Multi-Modal Data (a combined Image and Sensor model)

The entire framework is controlled by a single, comprehensive config.yml file, allowing for easy configuration of complex attack and defense scenarios.

Core Features
Multi-Dataset Support: Easily switch between binary images, multi-class images, and numerical sensor data.

Gradient Inversion Attacks: DLG, mDLG, GGL (GAN-based), GradInversion+ (batch reconstruction), and specialized versions for multi-modal data.

Integrity & Privacy Attacks: Model Poisoning and Data Poisoning (Backdoors).

Standard Defenses: Clipping, Differential Privacy, Median Aggregation, AES Encryption.

MyDefense Agent: A custom, adaptive defense that evaluates client updates for utility and data leakage to make real-time decisions.

Chaotic Obfuscation: A lightweight, reversible defense triggered by the MyDefense agent.

Project Structure
The project is organized into a modular structure to allow for easy extension and testing.

.
├── attacks/              # Contains all attack implementations
│   ├── ggl_attack.py
│   ├── gradient_inversion.py
│   └── ...
├── clients/              # Contains the client logic for each data type
│   ├── image_client.py
│   ├── sensor_client.py
│   └── multimodal_client.py
├── data/                 # (Created by scripts) Stores downloaded datasets
├── defense/              # Contains the custom defense logic
│   └── mydefense.py
├── models/               # (Created by scripts) Stores pre-trained GANs
├── server/               # Contains all server-side logic
│   ├── main.py           # Main entry point for the server
│   ├── strategy.py       # The core SecureFedAvg strategy (orchestrator)
│   ├── helpers.py        # Utility functions (testing, metrics, etc.)
│   └── reconstruction.py # The attack "switchboard"
├── utils_data/           # Contains data loading and preparation scripts
│   ├── casting_data_util.py
│   ├── cifar_data_util.py
│   └── ...
├── config.yml            # The master configuration file for all experiments
├── run_client.py         # The launcher script for clients
├── train_cifar_generator.py # Script to pre-train the GAN for CIFAR-10
└── prepare_cifar10_subset.py # Script to create a smaller dataset for testing
Module Connectivity & Workflow
The modules are designed to interact in a specific order, orchestrated by the server/strategy.py file.

Normal Training Round
The server (main.py -> strategy.py) initializes a global model based on config.yml.

The client launcher (run_client.py) starts a client (e.g., image_client.py).

The client receives the global model, trains it on its local data (from utils_data/), and sends the full, updated model weights back to the server.

The server (strategy.py) receives the updates and uses FedAvg to average them into a new global model.

MyDefense Agent Workflow (The Core Logic)
This is the most complex workflow, demonstrating how all the modules connect.

Client-Side:

A client (e.g., client1, the GI target) is configured to perform an attack. In its fit method, it calculates raw gradients (not a trained model) from a batch of its private data.

It saves the original data used to generate these gradients to a local .pkl file (e.g., client_data/client_1_image_data.pkl).

It sends the raw gradients and a special metric {"attack": "gradient_inversion"} to the server.

Another client (e.g., client2) might be instructed by the server to apply a defense, like chaotic_map_obfuscate (from crypto_utils.py), to its update.

Server-Side (strategy.py):

Analysis Phase: The aggregate_fit method begins.

It first checks for any client that sent the attack metric. For this client, it runs the reconstruction process.

It calls reconstruct_data (from reconstruction.py) on the raw, potentially defended update to see if the defense was effective.

The reconstruction function calls the specific attack (e.g., ggl_attack) from the attacks/ folder. The attack runs and returns a reconstruction_result.

The server loads the original data from the client's .pkl file.

Processing Phase:

The server then loops through all client updates and creates a clean version of each by calling decrypt_params or chaotic_map_deobfuscate (from crypto_utils.py) if needed.

Decision Phase:

The server now calls the MyDefenseAgent's decide_and_defend method (from defense/mydefense.py).

It passes both the clean update (for the utility check) and the reconstruction result (from the raw update, for the leakage check).

The decide_and_defend function uses helper functions (from defense/mydefense.py) to calculate the utility and leakage scores and returns a True/False decision.

Aggregation Phase:

The server collects all the clean updates that were accepted by the defense agent and aggregates them using FedAvg.

This cycle of attacking the defended data while aggregating the clean data allows for a comprehensive and realistic simulation of the security dynamics in Federated Learning.